Mesa's render-only driver (kmsro) for the Loongson LS7A1000: pair the display
controller, a PCI device driven by "loongson", with the etnaviv GPU beside it.
Upstream pairs only display devices on the platform bus with a render GPU, so
GBM and EGL on the display card fell back to llvmpipe and the X server's
glamor could not use the GC1000. Made against Mesa 26.1.5; applied by
../install-ls7a.sh with patch -p1 to the Mesa built into /opt/mesa-ls7a.

diff -ruN a/src/gallium/auxiliary/pipe-loader/pipe_loader_drm.c b/src/gallium/auxiliary/pipe-loader/pipe_loader_drm.c
--- a/src/gallium/auxiliary/pipe-loader/pipe_loader_drm.c
+++ b/src/gallium/auxiliary/pipe-loader/pipe_loader_drm.c
@@ -407,9 +407,17 @@
    pipe_loader_release(&dev, 1);
 
    /* For display-only devices that are not on the platform bus, we can't assume
-    * that any of the rendering devices are compatible. */
-   if (!is_platform_device)
-      return NULL;
+    * that any of the rendering devices are compatible. The exception is the
+    * display controller of Loongson's LS7A1000 bridge: a PCI device whose
+    * VRAM the GC1000 GPU beside it (etnaviv) draws into. */
+   if (!is_platform_device) {
+      char *kms_driver = loader_get_kernel_driver_name(kms_only_fd);
+      bool ls7a = kms_driver && strcmp(kms_driver, "loongson") == 0;
+
+      free(kms_driver);
+      if (!ls7a)
+         return NULL;
+   }
 
    /* For platform display-only devices, we try to find a render-capable device
     * on the platform bus and that should be compatible with the display-only
